# Binary assets must NEVER be line-ending-normalized.
#
# Git classifies a file as text when it contains no NUL bytes — but some binary
# files have none (e.g. a small, uncompressed signed PDF), so with
# core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout translates LF->CRLF and shifts every
# byte offset after the first newline. That silently corrupts byte-offset-
# sensitive formats: a signed PDF's xref/ByteRange no longer matches its stored
# location, and pyHanko's STRICT verify fails with "Could not find xref table at
# specified location" (pikepdf and pdf.js reconstruct a broken xref and hide the
# corruption, so only signature verification exposes it — it broke
# e2e-tests/specs/12-signatures.spec.ts on the GitHub windows runner, 5/5, while
# every other spec passed). Marking these `binary` disables EOL translation and
# textual diffing, so every checkout is byte-identical to the committed blob on
# all platforms.

*.pdf   binary
*.pdfx  binary
*.pfx   binary
*.p12   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.bmp   binary
*.sig   binary
*.dll   binary
*.exe   binary
*.pyd   binary
*.wasm  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.traineddata binary
# The committed ICC profiles under vendor/icc. Every one is sha256-pinned in
# scripts/icc-profiles.tsv and the distribution terms require them UNMODIFIED,
# so a checkout that translated one byte would both break the build gate and
# ship a profile we have no right to ship.
*.icc   binary

# The bundled trusted-list files record their own sha256 digests in their
# manifests, so a checkout that translates LF->CRLF makes every recorded digest
# wrong and the bundle no longer verifies as the artifact that was reviewed.
# `-text` disables EOL translation while keeping the textual diff a pinned
# artifact's review depends on. The pattern is `**`, not `*`: a gitattributes
# `*` does not cross a slash, so `*` reaches none of the files in a
# subdirectory of this tree.

src/engine/trust/** -text

# The pinned XMP extension schema templates. Their SHA-256 digests are recorded
# in the corpus MANIFEST.json and asserted against the file bytes, so the pins
# hash the exact committed blob — a checkout that translated LF->CRLF makes
# every pin wrong (it failed on the GitHub windows runner, where
# core.autocrlf defaults to true, while passing on a working tree that still
# held the LF bytes the fetch script wrote). `-text` disables EOL translation
# while keeping the textual diff a pinned artifact's review depends on.

tests/fixtures/xmp-extension-schemas/*.xml -text
